      },
      {
        "elements": [
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 4.415011037527593,
            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Involvement in Computer Science</strong><br>\nVon Neumann's interest in applied mathematics led him to work on problems of ballistics during World War II, which in turn introduced him to electronic computing. He wrote the seminal \"First Draft of a Report on the EDVAC,\" outlining the architecture of a stored-program computer. This design, later known as the von Neumann architecture, became fundamental to building modern computers.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The von Neumann Architecture</strong><br>\nThe von Neumann architecture is a computer design model that uses a single storage structure to hold both the set of instructions on how to perform the computation and the data required or generated by the computation. This concept revolutionized the way computers were made and is still the basis for most computer architectures today.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Work on the Manhattan Project</strong><br>\nDuring World War II, von Neumann worked on the Manhattan Project, contributing to the development of the atomic bomb. His mathematical expertise helped solve the implosion problem that was crucial to the bomb's design. He also worked on the development of hydrogen bombs and on various military projects involving computational models.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
