          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Later Years and Death</strong><br>\nIn his later years, von Neumann turned his attention to high-speed computing machines and contributed to the development of the Institute for Advanced Study's computer. He was diagnosed with cancer and passed away in 1957. Despite his illness, he continued to work on mathematics and computing until his final days.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy in Mathematics and Computer Science</strong><br>\nJohn von Neumann's legacy in mathematics and computer science is enormous. He is considered one of the greatest mathematicians of the 20th century. His work laid the groundwork for many areas of mathematics and the development of computer science as a field of study.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Personal Traits and Recognition</strong><br>\nKnown for his quick wit and love of parties, von Neumann's personality was as large as his intellect. He received numerous awards for his work, including the Medal for Merit for his wartime services. He was known for his ability to memorize complete books and for his speed of thought, often solving problems faster than his colleagues could comprehend.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
